WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.900
Backups are not one thing.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.840
A course backup can preserve selected course content and, depending on settings and permissions, may include different kinds of user data.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:20.620
A full site recovery plan must account for Moodle code, configuration, database, data storage, plugins, themes, and the environment needed to run them together.

4
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:27.920
Administrators should know which backup protects which risk, who can restore it, where it is stored, and how long it is retained.

5
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.240
A successful backup job is only evidence that a backup process produced an artifact.

6
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.780
It is not proof that the organization can recover.

7
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.160
Restore testing is the proof point administrators need.

8
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:52.340
Testing should happen in a controlled environment, with attention to permissions, privacy, storage, plugin availability, course behavior, links, enrolments, and user data choices.

9
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.400
A backup that cannot be restored in time does not meet the operational need.

10
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.920
Upgrade planning brings version awareness, change control, and recovery together.

11
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:10.420
Moodle 5.2 is the baseline for this course, and official guidance for direct upgrade to that branch requires starting from Moodle 4.4 or later.

12
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:24.120
Administrators should verify the deployed version, environment requirements, plugin compatibility, custom themes, backups, public-directory web-root behavior, and test results before touching production.

13
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.020
A rehearsal in staging is not wasted time; it is where surprises belong.

14
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.460
Rollback planning should be explicit.

15
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.920
If an upgrade fails after database changes, simply replacing files may not restore service.

16
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:48.460
The recovery plan should define the decision point, database backup, data storage backup, code state, plugin state, maintenance communication, and validation steps.

17
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.520
The plan should also say who can decide to proceed, pause, or roll back.

18
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.360
Performance administration starts with measurement.

19
00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:10.540
Slow pages may come from database load, storage latency, cache configuration, scheduled-task pressure, plugin behavior, theme complexity, large reports, mail delays, authentication systems, or client-side conditions.

20
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.240
Administrators should collect evidence before changing settings.

21
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:23.220
Capacity planning should consider course patterns, assessment windows, file usage, concurrent users, scheduled jobs, and support trends.

22
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:27.720
The most useful performance change is the one tied to an observed bottleneck.

23
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.600
Incident readiness is the operating model under stress.

24
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:48.980
When authentication fails, a plugin breaks after an update, mail stops, storage fills, privacy data is exposed, or a backup cannot restore, the organization needs roles, communication templates, escalation paths, evidence preservation, and recovery procedures.

25
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.640
The summary module turns these pieces into a repeatable Moodle administration lifecycle.
